WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.040
You don't want to sit
there and play solitaire.

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:04.200
Uh, you can discover new things.

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:10.410
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here again,
and today I want to talk about seniors.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:15.420
Seniors not only can learn languages,
they should learn languages

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:16.379
and I'm going to tell you why.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:20.220
So if you enjoy these videos,
please subscribe, click on

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.120
the bell for notification.

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:25.630
Uh, if you follow me on a
podcast, please leave a review.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:26.400
I appreciate it.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.759
Please notice that I've
changed my background here.

00:00:29.759 --> 00:00:34.920
I've now got this wood paneling
and I've sort of slid my, my

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:36.780
bookshelf off to the side.

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:38.610
People ask me if it's a real bookshelf.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:39.300
No, it's not.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:40.080
It's just a curtain.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:44.669
I slid it out to the side and now I'm
working off this wooden panelling.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.860
So the reason I'm talking about
seniors learning languages is because

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:54.070
I had an email from a good friend
of mine who plays hockey with me.

00:00:54.089 --> 00:00:57.839
He's one of our group of hockey
players that have been playing old

00:00:57.839 --> 00:01:03.540
timer's hockey since we were, I don't
know, over 40, so 30 years, 35 years.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:04.170
He's my age.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:07.770
He's 76 or 77 or 75 one of the two.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:11.910
And he says, Steve, um,
I want to learn Japanese.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:13.039
Can I get on your site?

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:15.710
I said, sure, absolutely.

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:18.980
Now some background, he has
been involved with Japan.

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:23.610
He coached hockey in Japan, in Sendai
for a year, and he's very fond of

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:28.520
Japan and so here he is, 76 years old,
he's going to start learning Japanese.

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:32.690
He can say, you know, the usual
konnichiwa, ohayo gozaimasu, but

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:34.220
he can't really speak Japanese.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.680
And I thought to myself, good for him.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:43.630
And here I am 76 and I'm learning my
Farsi and my Arabic, both Standard

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:48.990
and Egyptian Arabic, and I I've got
sort of Turkish waiting in the wings.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.940
And if I ever get to a proper level of
those, then I might explore Indonesian or

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:59.520
I might explore, uh, uh, you know, Urdu is
sort of a transition from, from Persian.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:03.380
Or I might go into Hindi and tackle
one of their writing systems.

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:07.140
Uh, or some other language, Swahili.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:08.820
I haven't learned an African language.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:10.320
I might tackle an African language.

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:12.540
All of that I have in front of me.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:17.010
Uh, next year, the year
after, the year after those

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:18.840
languages aren't going anywhere.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.270
I'm under no pressure to
learn those languages.

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:27.840
I don't need them for my job, but it's
just so much fun to contemplate that

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:29.880
I can go and learn these languages.

00:02:30.345 --> 00:02:34.575
And, you know, discover like I'm
now discovering, especially with the

00:02:34.575 --> 00:02:38.835
Persian, because I've got these wonderful
courses on Persian history that Sahra

00:02:38.895 --> 00:02:43.485
has, has prepared for, for LingQ,
which I enjoy going over repeatedly.

00:02:43.485 --> 00:02:48.735
And finally getting a sense of when
the Sasanian Dynasty was and when the

00:02:48.885 --> 00:02:56.010
Safavid Dynasty was and the Mongols
and the Turks and the uh, you know, the

00:02:56.010 --> 00:03:01.740
various, uh, influence of Rome and, and
then fighting with the Parthian Empire,

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:03.030
I guess they called it an English.

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:08.970
Um, and so for this one, so as a
senior, you know, here you are, uh,

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.140
you could be saying, well, you know,
I've, I've lived most of my life.

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:16.440
Uh, you know, I'm kind of,
uh, I mean, you don't want to

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:17.970
sit there and play solitaire.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:22.650
Uh, you can discover new things
particularly now with COVID where

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:27.240
it's not so easy to go traveling
around you can travel through your

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.820
language learning, uh, as a senior.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:34.620
And, and I don't think, you know,
as a senior, certainly I don't sit

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:38.160
down and learn this language and that
language, but I know that there are

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.200
a lot of languages there to learn.

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:43.800
And every language I have
learned has brought me so much.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:49.920
It's like being at a feast where there's
just this endless sort of table with

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:55.440
dishes and you can eat as many as you
want and just keep eating and enjoying.

00:03:55.529 --> 00:03:59.399
Of course, we know that for seniors
it's very good for their brains.

00:03:59.489 --> 00:04:04.859
So even if say my friend who hasn't
really been a language learner, like I've

00:04:04.859 --> 00:04:07.200
been a language learner, all his life.

00:04:07.769 --> 00:04:13.589
Uh, so maybe it's like the capacity
to learn languages is not as

00:04:13.589 --> 00:04:16.570
developed as it is in my brain
because I've been doing it more.

00:04:17.239 --> 00:04:22.815
But, um, it's like, if you haven't used a
muscle, you have to slowly start using it.

00:04:22.844 --> 00:04:27.435
Small steps, small steps, gradually
build up, build up the strength.

00:04:27.465 --> 00:04:30.914
And I think the same is true with
language learning regardless of your age.

00:04:31.155 --> 00:04:34.995
He might find it a little more difficult
because he's not as confident, he

00:04:34.995 --> 00:04:40.664
hasn't done it before, but I know
that anyone, if they, you know, gain

00:04:40.664 --> 00:04:44.864
that experience with the new language,
listening and reading, they can learn.

00:04:45.364 --> 00:04:51.855
And of course, especially today with the
range of content that's available online.

00:04:52.185 --> 00:04:57.775
So if we take, for example, he could
watch a Japanese series on Netflix

00:04:58.195 --> 00:05:01.545
and not understand a thing, except
that there would be subtitles.

00:05:01.635 --> 00:05:06.255
And through the subtitles, he would have
a sense of participating in an aspect of

00:05:06.255 --> 00:05:09.195
Japanese culture that is very stimulating.

00:05:09.195 --> 00:05:12.975
That encourages you,
entices you to do more.

00:05:13.335 --> 00:05:18.794
Now in order to get to the point where he
could understand those movies or even some

00:05:18.794 --> 00:05:24.945
of them, and which would then improve his
Japanese, he has to invest in the sort

00:05:24.945 --> 00:05:26.685
of beginner stuff like the mini stories.

00:05:26.745 --> 00:05:33.674
So there is a progression from repetitive
listening to relatively simple material

00:05:34.155 --> 00:05:38.174
to get yourself to a point where you
can derive more from these movies,

00:05:38.354 --> 00:05:40.205
but that's all there for you to.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:46.270
Those are all activities that as a
senior, you can commit one to three

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:51.120
hours a day to doing, and it takes
you on this sort of endless journey of

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:54.390
discovery initially for one language.

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:58.970
And that can be a few years I can tell you
from experience with Arabic and Persian,

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:03.630
and if you ever get tired of that journey,
then you can embark on another journey.

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:09.465
So, uh, not only is it, you know,
and I've, I've done videos before

00:06:09.465 --> 00:06:13.485
where I talk about age not being an
obstacle, but it's more than that.

00:06:13.485 --> 00:06:18.895
I think for seniors particularly who are
looking for meaningful things to do...

00:06:19.025 --> 00:06:19.255
Not...

00:06:19.255 --> 00:06:21.705
and many of them have their
meaningful activities.

00:06:21.705 --> 00:06:25.965
And I'm not saying that language
learning is the only meaningful activity.

00:06:25.965 --> 00:06:30.135
And of course there there's physical
activity and there's social activity

00:06:30.135 --> 00:06:33.360
and family activity, and there's
all kinds of things that can give

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:39.240
seniors a very full life, but I think
in particular language learning is

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.099
something that has a lot of benefits.

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:45.570
I'm not going to get into whether
it delays, you know, potential, you

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:49.440
know, Alzheimer's, there's all these
theories that it stimulates the brain

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:51.979
and protects it from dementia and so on.

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:53.190
I don't know if that's true.

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:55.140
Uh, it's an added benefit.

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:58.710
Uh, but I think the bigger thing is
that it's just such a rewarding thing

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:04.050
to do, something that seniors not only
can do but should do in my opinion.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:08.430
And, uh, I'll leave you with a couple
of videos that I did on the whole

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:10.650
issue of age and language learning.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:15.870
But again, seniors not only can
learn, but should learn languages.

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:17.040
Thank you for listening.

00:07:17.190 --> 00:07:17.730
Bye for now.

